      {
        type: "quiz",
        title: "Quick Check",
        question: "An infant with **Tetralogy of Fallot** suddenly becomes deeply cyanotic and irritable. What is the nurse's **first action**?",
        options: [
          { text: "Place the infant in the knee-chest position", correct: true },
          { text: "Administer IV morphine for agitation", correct: false },
          { text: "Begin chest compressions immediately", correct: false },
          { text: "Lay the infant flat with legs elevated", correct: false }
        ],
        explanation: "A **tet spell** is a sudden hypoxic episode in Tetralogy of Fallot. **Knee-chest position** (knees flexed to the chest) is the **first action** — it **increases systemic vascular resistance**, redirecting blood from the body back through the lungs to be oxygenated. Oxygen and morphine (which reduces pulmonary vascular resistance and calms the infant) are given after positioning. **Chest compressions** are only for pulseless arrest. **Flat with legs elevated** would worsen the shunt by decreasing SVR.",
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="42" font-weight="bold">Tet Spell First Action</text><rect x="100" y="150" width="900" height="220" rx="15" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.3" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="5"/><text x="550" y="225" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="44" font-weight="bold">KNEE-CHEST</text><text x="550" y="285" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="26">Increases systemic vascular resistance</text><text x="550" y="325" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="26" font-weight="bold">Redirects blood to lungs</text><rect x="100" y="400" width="900" height="280" rx="15" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.2" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Tet Spell Sequence</text><text x="130" y="500" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">1. Knee-chest position (FIRST)</text><text x="130" y="535" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">2. 100% oxygen blow-by</text><text x="130" y="570" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">3. Calm the infant (reduce O2 demand)</text><text x="130" y="605" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">4. Morphine if not relieving</text><text x="130" y="640" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">5. IV fluids for volume</text><rect x="100" y="710" width="900" height="330" rx="15" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="760" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Why Other Options Fail</text><text x="130" y="810" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Lay flat + legs up = decreases SVR</text><text x="130" y="850" fill="#888" font-size="20">Worsens right-to-left shunt</text><text x="130" y="895" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Morphine alone is not first-line</text><text x="130" y="935" fill="#888" font-size="20">Helps later if positioning insufficient</text><text x="130" y="980" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">CPR only if pulseless</text><text x="130" y="1020" fill="#888" font-size="20">Tet spell is hypoxic, not cardiac arrest</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Knee-chest is the first move — the physiology behind it."
        }
      },
      {
        type: "application",
        title: "Family Teaching and Long-Term Care",
        content: `**Acyanotic defect family teaching**: Most **VSDs close spontaneously by age 1**. Monitor for **CHF signs**: tachypnea during feeds, poor weight gain, sweating with feeds. Give small frequent feeds with extra burping. Endocarditis prophylaxis only for complex defects per current AHA guidelines.

**Cyanotic / Tetralogy family teaching**: Teach **knee-chest position** for tet spells at home. Avoid dehydration (thickens blood, worsens cyanosis). Surgical repair is usually completed in the first year. Lifelong cardiology follow-up.

**Sickle cell long-term**: Daily **penicillin prophylaxis** until age 5 (autosplenectomy risk). Stay hydrated, avoid cold, avoid high altitude, avoid extreme exertion. **Hydroxyurea** to increase HbF. Annual TCD ultrasound to screen for stroke risk. Update all immunizations including pneumococcal and meningococcal.

**Hemophilia A**: Recombinant **Factor VIII** prophylaxis or on-demand for bleeds. Lifestyle: **no contact sports**, helmet for biking, **soft-bristle toothbrush**, electric razor (when older), **MedicAlert bracelet**, **no IM injections, no ASA/NSAIDs**. Apply pressure to ANY bleeding site for at least 10 minutes. Joint bleeds (hemarthrosis) need RICE + Factor VIII; recurrent bleeds destroy joints.`,
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="70" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="38" font-weight="bold">Family Teaching Synthesis</text><rect x="60" y="120" width="980" height="220" rx="15" fill="#ec4899" opacity="0.2" stroke="#ec4899" stroke-width="3"/><text x="100" y="170" fill="#ec4899" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Acyanotic CHD (VSD/ASD/PDA)</text><text x="100" y="215" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">VSD often closes by age 1</text><text x="100" y="250" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Watch CHF: tachypnea/sweating with feeds</text><text x="100" y="285" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Small frequent feeds, extra burping</text><text x="100" y="320" fill="#10b981" font-size="22">Endocarditis prophy per AHA</text><rect x="60" y="360" width="980" height="220" rx="15" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.2" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="3"/><text x="100" y="410" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Tetralogy of Fallot</text><text x="100" y="455" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Teach knee-chest at home</text><text x="100" y="490" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Avoid dehydration</text><text x="100" y="525" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Surgical repair in first year</text><text x="100" y="560" fill="#10b981" font-size="22">Lifelong cardiology follow-up</text><rect x="60" y="600" width="980" height="220" rx="15" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="3"/><text x="100" y="650" fill="#ef4444" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Sickle Cell Long-Term</text><text x="100" y="695" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Penicillin daily until age 5</text><text x="100" y="730" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Hydration + avoid cold + altitude</text><text x="100" y="765" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Hydroxyurea to raise HbF</text><text x="100" y="800" fill="#10b981" font-size="22">Annual TCD stroke screen</text><rect x="60" y="840" width="980" height="200" rx="15" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.2" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="3"/><text x="100" y="890" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="28" font-weight="bold">Hemophilia A</text><text x="100" y="935" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22">Factor VIII prophy or on demand</text><text x="100" y="970" fill="#ef4444" font-size="22">No IM, no ASA/NSAIDs, no contact sports</text><text x="100" y="1005" fill="#10b981" font-size="22">MedicAlert + soft toothbrush + helmet</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Long-term family teaching priorities for cardiac and hematology patients."
        }
      }
