        type: "intro",
        title: "Phases of Crisis (Caplan, 1964)",
        content: `A **crisis** is a state in which usual coping mechanisms **fail** to resolve a stressful event. Gerald Caplan's classic model describes **four phases**:

**Phase 1 — Precipitating event**: A stressor occurs (loss, trauma, threat). Anxiety rises; the person tries familiar coping.

**Phase 2 — Vulnerable state**: Usual coping fails. Anxiety climbs further; the person tries new strategies (some helpful, some harmful).

**Phase 3 — Active crisis**: All coping has failed. **Severe anxiety**, **disorganization**, possibly panic. The person is most receptive to **outside intervention** here — the "**window of opportunity**" lasts ~4–6 weeks.

**Phase 4 — Resolution**: Either **adaptive** (return to baseline, possibly stronger) or **maladaptive** (new equilibrium at lower level, e.g., substance use, suicide, chronic mental illness).

**Crisis intervention is short-term (4–6 weeks), reality-focused, problem-solving** — not psychotherapy. The goal is to restore baseline functioning, not to explore underlying conflicts.`,
